    },
    {
      "name": "premium_economy_value_gap",
      "supports_focus": false,
      "rationale": "Concerns value-for-money in a specific cabin class, a pricing/value dimension rather than service quality."
    }
  ],
  "focus_coherence": 0.55,
  "structure_quality": 0.6,
  "focus_actionability": 0.75,
  "rationale": "The columns lean toward a service-experience focus — crew_service_quality, disruption_handling_quality, service_decline_signal, and special_meal_handling all sit on that axis, giving a plausible inferable centre. However, seat_hardware_condition, food_quality_assessment, and premium_economy_value_gap span distinct product/catering/value aspects, so the set reads partly as a general aspect-scan rather than a tightly focused decomposition; this weakens focus_coherence. Where they do cohere around service, the facets are well-differentiated (baseline quality, disruption handling, temporal decline, meal handling) at a useful granularity, supporting a moderate structure score. The implied service-quality focus is genuinely actionable — an analyst could investigate service consistency drivers of recommendation. The mixed scope keeps this from a strong pass on coherence."
}

